// No candidate proof, identity or official marking scheme is shipped in this report.
const trials = [
  {model:'Hosted Llama 3.3 70B · original reviewer',n:6,offered:0,exact:null,near:null,mae:null},
  {model:'Hosted Llama 3.3 70B · separate IMO partial-credit pilot',n:5,offered:0,exact:null,near:null,mae:null},
  {model:'Experimental hosted reviewer v2 · not used for student marks',n:3,offered:3,exact:0,near:0,mae:'5.67 / 7'},
  {model:'On-device Qwen3 1.7B',n:24,offered:12,exact:3,near:5,mae:'2.83 / 7'}
];
const counted=(num,n)=>num==null?'—':num+' / '+n;
export function benchmarkReport(){return `<section class="card spacer" aria-labelledby="benchmark-heading"><span class="eyebrow">MARKING STUDY · NOVEMBER 2026</span><h2 id="benchmark-heading">How close are the marks?</h2><p>Each proof was read twice without access to the human score. A model offered one mark only when both readings agreed on every criterion. Exact matches, marks within one point, and average absolute error are measured only on the proofs that received a mark.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Grader</th><th scope="col">Offered a mark</th><th scope="col">Exact</th><th scope="col">Within 1</th><th scope="col">Avg. error</th></tr></thead><tbody>${trials.map(t=>`<tr><th scope="row">${t.model}</th><td>${counted(t.offered,t.n)}</td><td>${counted(t.exact,t.offered)}</td><td>${counted(t.near,t.offered)}</td><td>${t.mae??'—'}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div><p>The original hosted reviewer returned no agreed marks on the six hard proofs. Ten of twelve readings returned a response; only two passed validation. The two individual marks were both 7/7, against human marks of 5 and 0. There is no exact-match rate when no marks were offered.</p><p>On a separate five-proof IMO-GradingBench pilot, the hosted reviewer offered no agreed marks, including on the three partially correct responses that humans scored 1, 4 and 5 out of 7. These were human assessments of model-written proofs. The accuracy of suggested marks is undefined when the grader abstains on every case.</p><p>The experimental hosted reviewer used numbered passages to locate its evidence. Both readings awarded 7/7 on three previously unseen responses. The human marks were <strong>1/7, 2/7 and 1/7</strong>. This is why the student-facing grader still uses the original reviewer. A separate earlier three-case attempt failed before any inference and is excluded from the table.</p><p>The local model abstained on 12 proofs, including one interrupted run. Of its six full-mark proposals, none matched the human score. One gave 7/7 against 0/7. Treat it as experimental feedback.</p><p class="tiny">The <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.36767v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TROJAN-Bench(H) study</a> provides researcher-written variants of six 2025 USAMO proofs and expert research marks from 0–7. These are not official grades on contestant scripts. The original hosted six are a subset of the device sample. The experimental three use different responses from the same six problems. Samples are small and should not be pooled or treated as a head-to-head accuracy ranking. No result here establishes accuracy on CMI, ISI or RMO exam papers.</p><h3>A proof with an official IMO result</h3><p>Both readings of the original hosted reviewer gave the <a href="https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/gemini/IMO_2025.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">published Gemini answer to IMO 2025 Problem 1</a> 7/7. This matches the item score supported by <a href="https://deepmind.google/blog/advanced-version-of-gemini-with-deep-think-officially-achieves-gold-medal-standard-at-the-international-mathematical-olympiad/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DeepMind’s five reported perfect answers and the officially confirmed 35/42 total</a>. We used our own rubric; a separately signed item marksheet was unavailable. This single full-credit model proof does not measure partial-credit accuracy.</p></section>`;}
